# Per-camera hardware settings for cam-01 (USB webcam) -> install as
# /etc/adapter/cameras/cam01.env (LAUNCH.md A9). OPTIONAL: with no file, the camera is
# found by detection and every setting below uses the default shown (tuned for the
# AVerMedia PW310). Read by adapter/bin/detect-hw.sh via camera-init.sh and
# publish-cam01.sh. KEY=VALUE only; environment variables override the file.

# Which camera. Leave both unset while only one USB camera is attached.
#CAM_MATCH=PW310                # substring of its /dev/v4l/by-id name
#CAM_DEVICE=/dev/v4l/by-id/...  # or pin the exact device (skips detection)

# What to ask the camera for. image/jpeg is hardware-decoded (v4l2jpegdec); a raw format
# such as YUYV needs no decoder and is normalised by v4l2convert instead. The decode stage
# follows this media type, so both work -- but MJPG carries far more pixels per USB byte,
# which is why the PW310 uses it at 720p. List what a camera actually offers with:
#   v4l2-ctl -d <device> --list-formats-ext
#CAPS=image/jpeg,width=1280,height=720,framerate=30/1
#CAPS=video/x-raw,format=YUY2,width=640,height=480,framerate=30/1

# The encode stage. CAM_FPS_OUT is what leaves videorate (the camera is asked for more in
# CAPS and frames are dropped to reach it); GOP is h264_i_frame_period.
# profile=high is deliberate and must not become baseline: GStreamer's v4l2h264enc
# negotiates Baseline unless told otherwise, and Baseline played in ffmpeg but rendered
# black in a browser (guide 16.1).
#CAM_FPS_OUT=15
#VIDEO_BITRATE=1000000
#GOP=30
#H264_PROFILE=high
#H264_LEVEL=4

# Microphone sample rate. NOT a quality setting: kvssink derives audio timestamps from a
# counter shared with the video track, so an audio frame must last at least as long as a
# video frame (guide 18.3). Keep AUDIO_RATE <= 1024 * CAM_FPS_OUT; publish-cam01.sh warns
# when it is not. Getting this wrong loses about half the audio, silently.
#AUDIO_RATE=16000

# camera-init.sh, applied in two passes: modes first (switch the automatics off), then the
# values those modes unlock. Names are the camera's own: v4l2-ctl -d <dev> --list-ctrls
# A control this camera does not have is skipped and reported, not fatal -- that is what
# makes swapping in a different webcam survivable. Note the driver CLAMPS an out-of-range
# value instead of refusing it (99999 became 6500 when tested), so a wrong number here is
# silently accepted: check with --get-ctrl rather than assuming it took.
#V4L2_MODE_CTRLS=auto_exposure=1,exposure_dynamic_framerate=0,white_balance_automatic=0,focus_automatic_continuous=0
#V4L2_VALUE_CTRLS=exposure_time_absolute=250,white_balance_temperature=4600,focus_absolute=120
